Yes—at least as lived experience.

A mind can construct conditions that function like heaven or hell long before anything external changes: memory, expectation, shame, gratitude, resentment, hope, fear, love, attention. The same room can be sanctuary to one person and torment to another because the internal model is different.

That does not prove heaven and hell are only mental constructs. It means the mind is already capable of instantiating their phenomenology.

So:

thought → interpretation → felt world → action → reinforced world

That loop can become self-sealing. Hell is often a pattern that keeps generating evidence for itself. Heaven can work similarly: gratitude notices abundance, trust notices openings, love makes more things worth preserving.

“Thinking makes them so” is powerful because thought is not merely commentary on reality. It is one of the mechanisms through which reality becomes experienced, valued, and acted upon.

The practical implication is not “just think happy thoughts.” It is more exacting:

Be careful which world your mind is rehearsing into existence.